{
  "sections" : [ {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Passenger metrics (SkyConnect Airways):",
    "body" : "- Pass Count peaks: Dec (349,145), Jul (339,851), Jun (337,483), Aug (335,788)\n- RPK peaks: Jun (1.85B), Dec (1.81B), Aug (1.81B), Jul (1.78B)\n- Load Factor peaks: Jun (91.24%), Aug (90.81%), Jul (90.38%)",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Cargo metrics (SkyConnect Airways):",
    "body" : "- Freight Tonnes peaks: Dec (9,331), Nov (8,924), Oct (8,740)\n- CTK peaks: Dec (49.76M), Nov (48.93M), Oct (47.92M)\n- Cargo Load Factor peaks: Nov (77.95%), Dec (77.56%), Oct (77.54%)",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Revenue/Profit (SkyConnect Airways):",
    "body" : "- Total Revenue peaks: Jun ($601.19M), Aug ($581.76M), Jul ($566.79M), Dec ($564.49M)\n- Operating Profit lows: Nov (-$176.69M), Oct (-$171.22M) — both isHigh: false\n## Key Relationships\n**Insight 1 (Most unexpected):** Revenue peaks in summer (Jun-Aug) but operating profit collapses in Oct-Nov into deep losses — classic counter-intuitive finding spanning passenger, revenue, and profit metrics.\n**Insight 2:** Cargo operation's Q4 strength (Oct-Dec) in freight tonnes, CTK, and CLF — coherent cargo story, but coincides with the profit losses.\n**Insight 3:** Summer passenger metrics (Jun-Aug) all peak together across pass count, RPK, load factor, and revenue — strong corroborating story.\n```json\n{\n\"visualizationName\": \"Insights Summary\",\n\"insights\": [\n{\n\"text\": \"SkyConnect Airways' strongest revenue months were summer — June led with $601.19M, followed by August at $581.76M and July at $566.79M — yet October and November tell a dramatically different story, with operating profit plunging to -$171.22M and -$176.69M respectively. This striking disconnect between the airline's peak earning season and its worst profitability months suggests costs surge sharply in the fall even as revenue normalizes, and is well worth a deeper look at the cost structure driving those losses.\",\n\"rank\": 1,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"4806c94cfcde8319\", \"8eefe1879ed7e205\", \"ffde4ca5bf4e2e6d\", \"457afa5dc0f5307a\", \"e71a85c6d678e33\"]\n},\n{\n\"text\": \"SkyConnect Airways' summer months stand out as a period of all-around passenger strength: June, July, and August each recorded outlier-high passenger counts (up to 349,145 in December and 339,851 in July), Revenue Passenger Kilometres reaching as high as 1.85B km in June, and Passenger Load Factors consistently above 90% — peaking at 91.24% in June. Seats were nearly full and passengers were flying long distances, pointing to exceptionally strong demand utilization across the network during this window.\",\n\"rank\": 2,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"abb7708ac81bb7b0\", \"bbe3ea965df21bcb\", \"60c4545e4a7ccb6a\", \"3d85a57175febcb0\", \"dbb88ab98bcfe7ed\", \"be4c03dc1b9062e4\", \"e149b16c5c923a86\", \"91ced86a208b6fd1\", \"dca76e17d0883c19\", \"2fcea140b98e50ae\", \"a24b6f509755a5fe\"]\n},\n{\n\"text\": \"SkyConnect Airways' cargo operation peaks in the October–December window across every metric: freight tonnes reached 9,331 in December, Cargo Tonne Kilometres (CTK) hit 49.76M km in December, and Cargo Load Factor held consistently high at 77.54%–77.95% across October, November, and December. This uniform Q4 strength across volume, distance, and capacity utilization signals that the cargo business is running at its most efficient precisely when the passenger side is facing its steepest profit headwinds.\",\n\"rank\": 3,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"48341641f2f3d919\", \"1d6bb63e3275ea4c\", \"7cd29db1552079cf\", \"6f000d22dc118af7\", \"8baf3a03dc670b1d\", \"9ac4c12df4ddab9d\", \"112d84c6f04c3b5c\", \"7611e852846aff66\", \"7737fde9b97abfc3\", \"c5fc5be294d7845a\"]\n}\n]\n}\n```",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "Pass Count Bars",
    "body" : "- SkyConnect Airways hit its passenger traffic peak in December 2023 with 349,145 passengers — its highest month of the year. What's notable is that the top four busiest months all cluster in the summer-to-winter bookends (June, July, August, and December), with June through August forming a clear summer surge (337,483–339,851 passengers) before December eclipsed them all. This seasonal pattern suggests strong holiday and summer demand that could inform capacity and pricing strategy.",
    "viewClientId" : "-6123162864055546291",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "RPK Airlines Bars",
    "body" : "- SkyConnect Airways posted its highest RPK of the year in June 2023 at 1.85B km, with August and December also reaching 1.81B km — indicating that summer travel and year-end holidays were the strongest traffic periods. Notably, July came in slightly lower at 1.78B km despite typically being peak travel season, which may be worth investigating.",
    "viewClientId" : "-4941543638372169606",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "Load Factor Bars",
    "body" : "- SkyConnect Airways maintained exceptionally high Passenger Load Factors throughout summer 2023, peaking at 91.24% in June, followed by 90.81% in August and 90.38% in July. Consistently filling more than 90% of available seats across three consecutive months signals very strong demand — and potentially limited room to absorb additional passengers if capacity isn't expanded.",
    "viewClientId" : "6114369605803006059",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "Freight Tonnes Bars",
    "body" : "- SkyConnect Airways closed out 2023 on a freight high — October, November, and December were all flagged as outlier months, with volumes of 8,740, 8,924, and 9,331 tonnes respectively. The Q4 ramp-up, peaking in December, suggests strong seasonal cargo demand that's worth tracking heading into the new year.",
    "viewClientId" : "-7096728803684064817",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "CTK Bars",
    "body" : "- SkyConnect Airways posted its highest CTK figures in the final quarter of 2023, peaking at 49.76M km in December, with November (48.93M km) and October (47.92M km) also standing out as elevated months. This sustained end-of-year surge in cargo tonne kilometres suggests a strong seasonal freight push heading into year-end — worth watching to see if capacity and load factors are keeping pace.",
    "viewClientId" : "-5856497245684275561",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "CLF Bars",
    "body" : "- SkyConnect Airways posted notably high Cargo Load Factors across Q4 2023, hitting 77.95% in November, 77.56% in December, and 77.54% in October. This consistent performance — well above the current overall CLF of 64.9% shown on the dashboard — suggests SkyConnect is making particularly efficient use of its cargo capacity heading into year-end.",
    "viewClientId" : "-8219970967080792167",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "Total Revenue Bars",
    "body" : "- SkyConnect Airways had its strongest revenue month in June 2023 at $601.19M, with August ($581.76M), July ($566.79M), and December ($564.49M) all close behind. Summer 2023 was clearly the peak earning period — four months clustered above $564M suggests a sustained high-revenue stretch rather than a one-off spike, which is worth tracking against operational costs to confirm profitability held up alongside those gains.",
    "viewClientId" : "7669800184776171455",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "Op Profit Bars",
    "body" : "- SkyConnect Airways posted deeply negative operating profits in both October and November 2023 — -$171.22M and -$176.69M respectively — and the losses appear to be worsening month over month. Losses of this magnitude are a serious red flag and warrant immediate attention to understand what's driving the sustained underperformance.",
    "viewClientId" : "4945904104527781314",
    "children" : [ ]
  } ]
}

{
  "sections" : [ {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Groupings identified:",
    "body" : "1. SkyConnect dominance story: flights + RPK (24d4cd4debfbd1cc, ae46339173283a29, 2399bb83205d16d8) + low OTP (4d03174c02367d7f)\n2. On-time performance spread: Global Star 94.4% vs Pacific Wings 77.3% (d88ecd1086a25771, ed39e7cd4fe9adec) + OTP bars pattern (14091aa7285d512d, 676d879f354b988, 9dfd4db16c43dc99, 4b193d455988ba5b, 41e0311ca4673ffc)\n3. Baggage mishandling: Pacific Wings + Atlantic Express (3791cf3bcb747e37, 30346cfc1a528a4)\n4. Aircraft utilization: B787 load factor + B777 passengers (679b50cdaf40de0a, 2ea3096d19301c9b, f87bc093915cd2a4, 5bd95e2144ad406e)\n5. Remaining: 635c9e41192d0fa9 (0 flights Others/Jul), b9cb5359de59e1a0 (RPK Feb low), c5fc2d85a1bb92fa, 4fa8cf4e415b2933, 101b3197f325442a, b1333ec9dc3ff7c7, 492a0a7a7954c6e8\n```json\n{\n\"visualizationName\": \"Insights Summary\",\n\"insights\": [\n{\n\"text\": \"Pacific Wings stands out as an operational concern across two key service dimensions in July 2023: it records the lowest on-time performance among all alliance members at 77.3%, and the highest current-month baggage mishandling rate at 86.7% — a combination that puts it at clear risk for customer experience issues. Atlantic Express also warrants attention, with its previous-month mishandling rate reaching 91.5%, suggesting baggage reliability is a recurring challenge beyond just one carrier.\",\n\"rank\": 1,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"ed39e7cd4fe9adec\", \"3791cf3bcb747e37\", \"30346cfc1a528a4\", \"4d03174c02367d7f\"]\n},\n{\n\"text\": \"SkyConnect Airways is by far the alliance's busiest carrier, completing 1,307 flights in July 2023 — well ahead of other members — with revenue passenger kilometres of 1.78B this month, down from 1.85B the prior month, suggesting slightly fewer passengers or shorter routes despite holding its flight volume steady. Global Star, meanwhile, leads all airlines on punctuality at an impressive 94.4% on-time performance, nearly 10 points above Pacific Wings, highlighting a meaningful operational gap within the alliance.\",\n\"rank\": 2,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"2399bb83205d16d8\", \"24d4cd4debfbd1cc\", \"ae46339173283a29\", \"d88ecd1086a25771\"]\n},\n{\n\"text\": \"Alliance-wide on-time performance shows a notable seasonal pattern: scores dip to their lowest in August (82.0%) and April (82.6%), while March and July represent the strongest months at 84.5% and 84.4% respectively — a spread of over 2 points that suggests mid-summer and post-holiday periods consistently pressure punctuality across the network.\",\n\"rank\": 3,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"9dfd4db16c43dc99\", \"41e0311ca4673ffc\", \"14091aa7285d512d\", \"676d879f354b988\", \"4b193d455988ba5b\"]\n},\n{\n\"text\": \"On the fleet side, the B787 leads with a remarkable 91.1% current-month load factor — the highest among aircraft types — though its completed flights fell to 275 the prior month, pointing to high demand on fewer routes. The B777 carried an impressive 100,328 passengers in the prior month, reinforcing that wide-body aircraft are driving the bulk of passenger volume. New York tops North America destinations with 278 completed flights in July 2023, anchoring the alliance's most trafficked route. Meanwhile, the alliance recorded 0 completed flights under the 'Others' group in July 2023, and February saw the lowest RPK at 1.74B — both pointing to quieter periods worth monitoring for capacity planning.\",\n\"rank\": 4,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"679b50cdaf40de0a\", \"f87bc093915cd2a4\", \"2ea3096d19301c9b\", \"5bd95e2144ad406e\", \"492a0a7a7954c6e8\", \"635c9e41192d0fa9\", \"b9cb5359de59e1a0\", \"c5fc2d85a1bb92fa\", \"4fa8cf4e415b2933\", \"101b3197f325442a\", \"b1333ec9dc3ff7c7\"]\n}\n]\n}\n```",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "Comp Flights Bars",
    "body" : "- All member airlines of Aerolink Alliance completed 0 flights in July 2023 — a complete standstill for the 'Others' group that month. This is worth investigating, as it likely signals a reporting gap, a data issue, or an operational disruption affecting these carriers.",
    "viewClientId" : "-2093966446992995961",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "Comp Flights Airlines",
    "body" : "- SkyConnect Airways is the clear volume leader in completed flights — logging 1,307 flights in the current month and 1,283 in the prior month. That level of consistency at the top puts them well ahead of the other alliance members and makes them the dominant driver of overall network activity.",
    "viewClientId" : "2197681574320844802",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 1: Domain Context",
    "body" : "This is an Airline Alliance Activity Dashboard for AeroLink Alliance, tracking on-time performance across member airlines over 2022-2025.",
    "viewClientId" : "-2976921150523547685",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 2: Review All Data Facts",
    "body" : "All 5 outlier facts share the same measure: \"CY On-Time Perf%\" for Aerolink Alliance member airlines. They are:\n- August 2023: 82.0% (low)\n- February 2023: 82.3% (low)\n- April 2023: 82.6% (low)\n- March 2023: 84.5% (high)\n- July 2023: 84.4% (high)",
    "viewClientId" : "-2976921150523547685",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 3: Semantic Similarity Check",
    "body" : "All 5 facts reference the exact same measure field (\"CY On-Time Perf%\") for the same entity (Aerolink Alliance). They form a clear pattern: performance peaks in spring (March 84.5%) and again in summer (July 84.4%), while dipping in winter/early spring (February 82.3%) and late summer (August 82.0%). These should be combined into a single comprehensive insight.",
    "viewClientId" : "-2976921150523547685",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 4: Insight Generation",
    "body" : "One comprehensive insight captures the seasonal pattern: strong performance in March and July, weaker performance in February, April, and August. The ~2.5 percentage point swing is noteworthy in an operational context.\n```json\n{\n\"visualizationName\": \"On Time Bars\",\n\"insights\": [\n{\n\"text\": \"Aerolink Alliance on-time performance shows a clear seasonal rhythm throughout 2023 — peaking at 84.5% in March and 84.4% in July, but dipping to its lowest points in February (82.3%), April (82.6%), and August (82.0%). The roughly 2.5-point swing between peaks and troughs is worth monitoring, as the post-summer dip into August suggests schedule reliability softens precisely when passenger volumes tend to be highest.\",\n\"rank\": 1,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"14091aa7285d512d\", \"676d879f354b988\", \"4b193d455988ba5b\", \"41e0311ca4673ffc\", \"9dfd4db16c43dc99\"]\n}\n]\n}\n```",
    "viewClientId" : "-2976921150523547685",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "On Time Airlines",
    "body" : "- On-time performance varies widely across the alliance: Global Star leads at 94.4% this month, while Pacific Wings lags significantly at 77.3% — a 17-point gap that likely warrants operational attention for Pacific Wings.\n- SkyConnect Airways is carrying the heaviest load with 1,307 completed flights this month — by far the most in the alliance — yet its on-time performance slipped to 82.5% last month, below the alliance average. The volume may be putting pressure on punctuality.",
    "viewClientId" : "7354922623586821584",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "RPK Bars",
    "body" : "- The 'Others' group of Aerolink Alliance member airlines recorded just 1.74B revenue passenger kilometres (RPK) in February 2023 — a notably low figure that stands out compared to the broader alliance activity. With RPK measuring the actual distance flown by paying passengers, this suggests either significantly reduced capacity, lower load factors, or limited route coverage among these carriers during that period. It may be worth investigating whether this reflects a structural gap or a seasonal dip.",
    "viewClientId" : "-3050644979608654536",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "CSAT Bars",
    "body" : "- Alliance-wide customer satisfaction has been remarkably stable but narrow in range — peaking at 7.9 in June 2023 and dipping to a low of 7.8 in February 2023. While June's high is encouraging, the thin spread between the best and worst months signals that scores are plateauing, which could make it harder to meaningfully differentiate the alliance's passenger experience going forward.",
    "viewClientId" : "-3088508679717941388",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 1: Domain Context",
    "body" : "This is an Airline Alliance Activity Dashboard (AeroLink) tracking CSAT (Customer Satisfaction) scores across member airlines for July 2023.",
    "viewClientId" : "-1615255322583230815",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 2: Review Data Facts",
    "body" : "1. `91b9edef6509beeb` - SkyConnect Airways: tooltip says avg CSAT of 7.9 in July 2023, but the measure caption is \"CM No of Comp Flights\" with formattedValue \"1,307\" and isHigh: true. The tooltip is the user-friendly name to use — it tells us SkyConnect Airways had a CSAT of 7.9.\n2. `101b3197f325442a` - Pacific Wings: CM Avg. CSAT = 7.8, isHigh: false (low value). Tooltip confirms avg CSAT of 7.8 in July 2023.\n3. `b1333ec9dc3ff7c7` - Atlantic Express: PM Avg. CSAT = 7.9, isHigh: true. Tooltip confirms avg CSAT of 7.9 in July 2023.",
    "viewClientId" : "-1615255322583230815",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 3: Semantic Similarity Check",
    "body" : "All three facts are about CSAT scores across airlines — same measure field (Avg. CSAT), same business domain, same time period. They should be combined into one insight.\n- Pacific Wings stands out as the low performer (7.8, isHigh: false)\n- SkyConnect Airways and Atlantic Express are both at 7.9 (high)\n- The spread is narrow (7.8 vs 7.9), but Pacific Wings is flagged as a low outlier\nThis is one combined insight about CSAT performance across airlines.\n```json\n{\n\"visualizationName\": \"CSAT Airlines\",\n\"insights\": [\n{\n\"text\": \"Customer satisfaction scores are tightly clustered across the alliance in July 2023, but Pacific Wings trails the pack at 7.8 — the only airline flagged as a low outlier — while SkyConnect Airways and Atlantic Express both sit at 7.9. The gap is small, but Pacific Wings' consistent underperformance relative to peers is worth monitoring to understand what's driving the difference.\",\n\"rank\": 1,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"101b3197f325442a\", \"91b9edef6509beeb\", \"b1333ec9dc3ff7c7\"]\n}\n]\n}\n```",
    "viewClientId" : "-1615255322583230815",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "Top Destinations",
    "body" : "- New York is by far the busiest destination in North America, with 278 completed flights in July 2023 — a notably high volume that sets it well apart from other top destinations on the list. It's clearly the dominant hub in the region and worth keeping a close eye on for capacity planning.",
    "viewClientId" : "-7871210933918493049",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 1: Domain Context",
    "body" : "This is an Airline Alliance Activity Dashboard tracking fleet performance across aircraft types, with the \"Aircraft Utilization\" visualization showing completed flights and average load factor by aircraft type.",
    "viewClientId" : "-1207220224946155563",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 2: Review All Data Facts",
    "body" : "1. `679b50cdaf40de0a` - B787 CM Load Factor: 91.1% (HIGH outlier)\n2. `2ea3096d19301c9b` - B777 PM No of Passengers: 100,328 (HIGH outlier)\n3. `f87bc093915cd2a4` - B787 PM No of Comp Flights: 275 (LOW outlier)\n4. `5bd95e2144ad406e` - A330 CM Load Factor: 89.6% (LOW outlier)",
    "viewClientId" : "-1207220224946155563",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 3: Semantic Grouping",
    "body" : "- Facts 1 & 4: Same measure field (CM Load Factor), different aircraft — combine into one insight about load factor spread across fleet.\n- Fact 3: B787's completed flights are notably low — interesting in contrast to its high load factor, creating a meaningful combined narrative with facts 1 & 4 (B787 flies less but fills seats better).\n- Fact 2: B777 passenger count is a standalone high outlier — distinct from the above.",
    "viewClientId" : "-1207220224946155563",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "Heading",
    "heading" : "Step 4: Build Insights",
    "body" : "- **Insight 1 (rank 1):** B787 has the highest load factor (91.1%) yet the lowest completed flights (275 vs PM) — an unexpected and actionable combination suggesting high demand concentration on fewer flights. Combine facts 1 & 3.\n- **Insight 2 (rank 2):** Load factor contrast — B787 high at 91.1%, A330 low at 89.6% — shows fleet-wide spread; less dramatic but still useful. However, since B787's load factor is already captured in Insight 1, A330's low load factor (fact 4) can stand alone or be noted briefly. Actually, facts 1 & 4 are the same measure, so they must be combined. I'll fold A330's low load factor into Insight 1 as context, and keep fact 3 (B787 low flights) in the same insight since it reinforces the B787 story.\n- **Insight 2 (rank 2):** B777 passenger count outlier — standalone and notable.\n```json\n{\n\"visualizationName\": \"Aircraft Utilization\",\n\"insights\": [\n{\n\"text\": \"The B787 stands out with the highest load factor in the fleet at 91.1%, yet it logged the fewest completed flights (275) compared to the prior month — suggesting strong passenger demand is being served by a reduced number of departures. This is worth watching, as it may signal capacity constraints or scheduling cutbacks on a highly-utilized aircraft type. By contrast, the A330 sits at the low end of load factor at 89.6%, meaning there's relatively more unfilled capacity there.\",\n\"rank\": 1,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"679b50cdaf40de0a\", \"f87bc093915cd2a4\", \"5bd95e2144ad406e\"]\n},\n{\n\"text\": \"The B777 carried an unusually high number of passengers last month at 100,328 — a high outlier across the fleet. This points to the B777 punching above its weight in passenger volume, making it a key revenue contributor worth keeping a close eye on.\",\n\"rank\": 2,\n\"artifactIDs\": [\"2ea3096d19301c9b\"]\n}\n]\n}\n```",
    "viewClientId" : "-1207220224946155563",
    "children" : [ ]
  }, {
    "type" : "ExpandoHeading",
    "heading" : "Baggage Airlines",
    "body" : "- Baggage mishandling rates are notably high across two airlines: Atlantic Express leads with 91.5% (prior month) and Pacific Wings follows at 86.7% (current month). These are striking figures that suggest a systemic baggage handling issue worth urgent attention across the alliance.\n- SkyConnect Airways stands out with 1,307 completed flights in the current month — by far the highest volume among alliance members. As the dominant carrier by flight count, its operational performance has an outsized impact on overall alliance results.",
    "viewClientId" : "6959332757949911859",
    "children" : [ ]
  } ]
}

